"Combines a half-density, raw observation strip, and concise interval summary to expose complementary aspects of a distribution.\n\nUse when: Readers need to inspect shape, individual observations, and a group summary together. Group sizes remain small enough for a readable raw-data layer.\n\nAvoid when: A compact single-purpose display would answer the question more clearly.\n\nVisual grammar: Half-violin width encodes density along the measured axis. Dots encode raw values; an offset box or interval encodes the chosen summary.\n\nBuild: Align half-density, raw strip, and summary on one numeric scale. Offset the three layers perpendicular to that scale so they remain distinct. Name the interval convention, density bandwidth, and group sample sizes.\n\nCheck: Check every layer uses the same included observations and units. Verify jitter changes only the nonnumeric coordinate. Confirm density and interval summaries are calculated independently and correctly.\n\nPitfalls: Overlaid components can conceal each other; use separated lanes with consistent spacing. A box, standard deviation, and confidence interval have different meanings and require explicit labels.\n\nExample: Compare synthetic completion times across two groups using a half-density, individual dots, and a median box.\n\nAlternatives: Violin plot, Strip plot, Box plot."
